// The BASE prelude — the ambient scope every vilan package resolves under unless
// its manifest says otherwise (`prelude.md` §5.1). Eight names, each admitted by a
// rule and not by rank alone: universal (base layer, reachable on every target),
// language-level (the language itself produces it, or every program uses it
// whatever its domain), unambiguous (exactly one std definition claims the name),
// and top-of-census in more than one corpus.
//
// `Option` is the strongest case: `Option<T>` is a lang item, manufactured by `?.`
// and by view-returning lookups, so a program can be handed one without ever naming
// its type — and then, before this module, could not take it apart. `Result`
// travels with it as the `!` operator's canonical type. `print` is the one name a
// program needs before it can be observed at all.
//
// `Iterator` joined in Order 46 (B535, ruled): a trait's methods resolve only
// where the trait is in scope (B515), and every iterator chain — `filter`, `map`,
// `fold`, `to_list` — is `Iterator`'s defaults, so leaving it out would have put
// `import std::iterator::Iterator;` at the head of nearly every program once
// B515's warning becomes an error. It is language-level by the rule above: `for`
// and every collection's `iter()` hand one over.
//
// This module is nothing but re-exports, and that is the rule rather than an
// accident: a prelude's ambient names are exactly the names it publishes, so a
// reader answers "what is in scope here" by reading this file.
//
// Selected as `prelude = "std::prelude"`, which is also what omitting the key
// means. The web set is next door in `web.vl`.
export import pkg::debug::dbg;
export import pkg::io::print;
export import pkg::iterator::Iterator;
export import pkg::option::Option::{ self, None, Some };
export import pkg::result::Result::{ self, Err, Ok };
